Right. Death can teach observers, but it cannot teach the dead through the consequence once the learner is gone.

That exposes a boundary in our feedback model:

\text{Action}\rightarrow\text{Consequence}\rightarrow\text{Feedback}\rightarrow\text{Learning}

requires the learning system to survive the consequence long enough to receive the feedback.

If the consequence destroys the learner:

\text{Action}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{terminal consequence}}
\rightarrow \cancel{\text{feedback to actor}}

the individual loop is broken.

But the information doesn’t necessarily disappear. It can jump levels:

\text{Individual failure}
\rightarrow
\text{Witness}
\rightarrow
\text{Story}
\rightarrow
\text{Collective memory}
\rightarrow
\text{changed behavior}

So death reveals two distinct learning channels:

Experiential learning: I experienced the consequence and updated.

Vicarious/collective learning: Someone else experienced the consequence; I received its afterglow and updated without paying the full price.

That’s arguably one of storytelling’s deepest functions. It lets consequences travel farther than the person who experienced them.

And there’s an important design principle for the Pantheon:

\boxed{\text{Prefer informative failure over terminal failure.}}

An experiment should ideally produce enough consequence to reveal something while preserving the experimenter’s ability to observe, update, and try again.

That’s why simulations, reversible experiments, prototypes, drills, stories, historical case studies, and small bets are so valuable: they can capture portions of the lesson while reducing the price of acquiring it.

So perhaps the broader primitive is:

\boxed{\text{Learnability}=
\text{Consequence}
\times
\text{Observability}
\times
\text{Survivability}
\times
\text{Retention}}

Too little consequence → weak signal.

Too much consequence → no learner.

The productive region lies between them.

And that gives the Rocky quote another layer: getting hit isn’t sufficient. Remaining capable of moving afterward is what allows the hit to become information rather than merely damage.

Survival doesn’t automatically make you wiser.

But survival preserves the possibility of becoming wiser from what happened.